.TH BLK 3
.SH NAME
blk_open, blk_close, blk_info, blk_get, blk_put, blk_putf, blk_read, blk_write, blk_discard \- swappable memory blocks on the SD card
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.ft B
#include <blk.h>

int blk_open(unsigned long \fIbytes\fP, int \fIflags\fP)
int blk_close(void)
int blk_info(struct blkinfo *\fIbi\fP)
int blk_get(unsigned long \fIid\fP, void *\fIbuf\fP)
int blk_put(unsigned long \fIid\fP, const void *\fIbuf\fP)
int blk_putf(unsigned long \fIid\fP, const void *\fIbuf\fP, int \fIflags\fP)
int blk_read(unsigned long \fIid\fP, unsigned \fIoff\fP, void *\fIbuf\fP, unsigned \fIlen\fP)
int blk_write(unsigned long \fIid\fP, unsigned \fIoff\fP, const void *\fIbuf\fP, unsigned \fIlen\fP)
int blk_discard(unsigned long \fIid\fP, unsigned long \fIn\fP)
.fi
.ft P
.SH DESCRIPTION
A process can keep working data that does not fit in RAM in numbered
blocks of
.B BLK_SIZE
(4096) bytes on the SD card's swap partition.
The memory manager moves them between the card and buffers in the
process's own data or stack segment; nothing is mapped.
Blocks are there only while the system has them on
.RB ( blk=on
in
.BR /etc/rc.conf ,
or
.BR "swapon /dev/hd3 [plain] blk" ).
.PP
.B blk_open()
reserves
.I bytes
of block space, rounded up to whole extents (see
.BR blk_info ),
and returns the number of blocks reserved.
Blocks 0 to N\-1 then exist and read as zeros until written.
With
.B BLK_ANY
a request larger than the largest free run gets that run instead.
A second call with a smaller size gives the tail back; a larger one grows
the run in place if the extents after it are free, otherwise it fails with
.B ENOSPC
and the old run stays.
A process that is not root holds at most half of the block space.
.PP
The reservation ends with
.BR blk_close() ,
.B exec
or exit; nothing is written then, and no other process can ever read the
old blocks: they read as zeros.
A forked child starts without a reservation.
.PP
.B blk_info()
fills
.B struct blkinfo
with the caller's reservation
.RB ( blocks ),
the largest free run
.RB ( largest ),
the whole block space
.RB ( space )
and the blocks per extent
.RB ( extent ),
all in blocks.
.PP
.B blk_get()
reads block
.I id
into the 4096 bytes at
.IR buf ;
.B blk_put()
writes them.
.B blk_putf()
with
.B BLK_IFCHANGED
writes nothing if the data on the card is already the same (sector CRCs),
which saves card wear for an app that writes back whole blocks it may not
have changed.
.B blk_read()
and
.B blk_write()
move a part of a block:
.I off
and
.I len
are multiples of
.B BLK_SECT
(512) and
.IR off + len
is at most 4096.
.PP
.B blk_discard()
says that blocks
.I id
to
.IR id + n \- 1
are not needed any more.
Their contents are undefined from then on (zeros, or the old data).
The card is told at idle (SD DISCARD) if it can take it; otherwise
nothing happens.
.PP
A request from a process in the background waits until the system is
idle; one from the foreground app is served at once.
.SH "RETURN VALUE"
0 (or the number of blocks for
.BR blk_open ),
or \-1 with
.I errno
set.
.SH ERRORS
.TP 10
.B ENOSYS
Blocks are off.
.TP
.B EBADF
No reservation.
.TP
.B ERANGE
A block or sector range outside the reservation.
.TP
.B ENOSPC
No free run large enough, or more than half of the space (not root).
.TP
.B EIO
A sector failed its CRC or could not be read; the other sectors are not
affected.
.TP
.B ENXIO
The card is out; try again later.
The process is not killed.
.TP
.B EPERM
A set-uid or set-gid program, or a
.B "Secret: yes"
package, while swap is plain (unencrypted).
.TP
.B EINVAL
A bad buffer, offset or length.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR libblk (3),
.BR swapon (8).
.SH NOTES
The data is on the card unencrypted in plain mode; each sector carries a
CRC-32, so damage is found, not hidden.
The design is in the spec, section 12h (R12.34 to R12.40).
